 ## Overview
-The Voxel Shading Pipeline is a fragment-shader-based lighting model for ray-marched voxel worlds that combines Lambertian diffuse shading, rim lighting, indirect ambient reflection, and a compact microfacet specular term [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of providing visually plausible surface lighting for discrete voxel geometry without requiring explicit mesh generation or complex global illumination [S2, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods by integrating hard shadow casting and emissive block handling directly into the ray-marched hit loop, whereas [GGX Microfacet BRDF](3715-ggx-microfacet-brdf.md) focuses on analytical surface scattering and [Fast Shading for Voxel Engines](5606-fast-shading-for-voxel-engines.md) optimizes for engine integration [S1, S2, unofficial].
+The Voxel Shading Pipeline is a per-voxel lighting model that computes illumination for surface points identified by ray marching through a voxel grid [S1, S2, unofficial]. It combines Lambertian diffuse reflection, rim lighting, indirect ambient contribution, and a microfacet-based specular term, modulated by hard shadows and tone mapping [S1, unofficial]. It differs from continuous surface shading by operating on discrete voxel coordinates, often using the voxel face normal directly rather than interpolating across a mesh, and typically employs hard shadows due to the binary nature of voxel occupancy [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** Camera position (`camPos`), ray direction (`rayDir`), ray hit result (`tr` containing `hit`, `t`, `cell`, `normal`), block type (`b`), sun direction (`L`), and time (`time`) for sun rotation [S1, unofficial].
-**Outputs:** Final pixel color (`finalColor`) [S1, unofficial].
+**Inputs:**
+- Camera position $\mathbf{c}$ and ray direction $\mathbf{v}_{ray}$ [S1, unofficial].
+- Voxel grid data: block type, albedo texture, emissive intensity [S1, unofficial].
+- Light direction $\mathbf{L}$ (sun vector) [S1, S2, unofficial].
+- Parameters: `lightColor`, `ambient`, `roughness`, `F0`, `tLimit`, `maxSteps` [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Outputs:**
+- Final pixel color $\mathbf{C}_{final}$ [S1, unofficial].
 
