-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Precision:** Use `1e-5` offset in ray origin to avoid self-intersection artifacts [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Shadow Bias:** Use `N * 0.01` offset for shadow ray origin [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Division by Zero:** Add `1e-6` to denominators in specular calculation [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Overbright:** Atmosphere sky color is exponentiated negatively to reduce brightness [S1, unofficial].
+**Parameters:**
+- `lightColor`: RGB $(1.0, 0.96, 0.85)$ [S1, unofficial].
+- `ambient`: RGB $(0.12, 0.14, 0.18)$ [S1, unofficial].
+- `roughness`: $0.54$ [S1, unofficial].
+- `F0`: $0.14$ [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Edge Cases:**
+- If $t \le 0$, the hit position is set to the camera position [S1, unofficial].
+- Precision issues in denominator of specular term are guarded with $\epsilon = 10^{-6}$ [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** O(1) per pixel for shading, plus O(N) for shadow ray marching where N is steps to sun [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** O(1) per pixel, plus texture memory for albedo [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Interactive on modern GPUs due to simple shading model and hard shadows [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, visually pleasing for voxel worlds, handles emissive blocks [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Hard shadows only, no soft shadows or global illumination [S1, S2, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of steps in ray marching, plus one additional trace for shadowing if enabled [S1, S2, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ per pixel, assuming the voxel grid is accessible via hashing or uniform sampling [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Simple to implement; hard shadows are cheap in voxel grids; fits the aesthetic of block-based worlds [S2, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Hard shadows can appear jagged; specular highlights are per-voxel, potentially causing aliasing at grazing angles [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method is documented in community shader repositories and graphics programming blog posts detailing ray-marched voxel rendering techniques [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is applied in indie voxel games and educational graphics projects to achieve real-time lighting on discrete voxel geometry [S1, S2, unofficial].
+The method originates from interactive voxel rendering techniques popularized in indie game development and educational ray marching projects [S1, S2, unofficial]. It adapts standard PBR concepts (GGX, Fresnel) to the discrete voxel domain, often simplifying geometry to face normals [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
